125, -0.0189056396484375, -0.0185089111328125, -0.0116729736328125, 0.03350830078125, 0.0139617919921875, -0.0094757080078125, 0.0406494140625, -0.02392578125, 0.0205078125, 0.004390716552734375, 0.0137939453125, 0.033203125, 0.005275726318359375, 0.03668212890625, -0.0112152099609375, 0.034271240234375, 0.016387939453125, 0.00988006591796875, -0.004730224609375, -0.0091094970703125, -0.00995635986328125, -0.031768798828125, 0.00565338134765625, -0.00693511962890625, -0.01181793212890625, 0.01451873779296875, 0.043731689453125, -0.029541015625, -0.0187530517578125, -0.01023101806640625, 0.0086212158203125, -0.0056915283203125, -0.006786346435546875, 0.02655029296875, 0.022186279296875, -0.0007925033569335938, -0.01078033447265625, -0.0196380615234375, -0.01342010498046875, 0.0010366439819335938, -0.023956298828125, 0.01042938232421875, -0.033599853515625, -0.004558563232421875, -0.0011310577392578125, -0.008544921875, 0.00689697265625, 0.00429534912109375, 0.002960205078125, -0.0240020751953125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines often optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, missing opportunities to leverage shared structure across tasks. A key challenge is not only curating each dataset, but also organizing diverse supervision according to the dependencies among generative capabilities.\n\n**Overview and Key Concepts**\n\nWe introduce a capability-driven data infrastructure\u2014a framework that builds and schedules data according to the progression of model capabilities. In this context, a **capability** is a distinct generative skill, such as text-to-image synthesis (generating images from text prompts), image editing (modifying images based on instructions), or associating images with external knowledge (linking images to structured entities). **Curriculum scheduling** refers to sequencing training data so that the model learns foundational capabilities before progressing to more complex ones. **Caption experts** are automated or human-in-the-loop systems that generate and harmonize image descriptions and entity tags across tasks, ensuring consistency and semantic alignment. The **harmonization module** is a system component that standardizes and aligns captions and entity tags across different data sources and tasks, producing a unified, consistent dataset for training.\n\nAdditional key terms include:\n\n- **Capability dependencies:** Relationships indicating that mastering one capability (e.g., text-to-image) facilitates learning another (e.g., image editing). For example, a model that can generate images from text is better positioned to learn how to edit those images based on instructions.\n- **Capability milestones:** Predefined performance thresholds (e.g., FID < 10, CLIP score > 0.28) that indicate when a model has sufficiently acquired a capability to progress to more advanced tasks.\n\n**Workflow Summary**\n\nThe workflow, previously depicted in Figure 1, proceeds as follows:\n\n1. **Data engines** generate and curate supervision for each capability.\n2. **Caption experts** harmonize descriptions and entity tags across all data.\n3. The **harmonization module** integrates these outputs, producing a unified, consistent dataset.\n4. The **curriculum scheduler** sequences data exposure based on capability dependencies and model progress, dynamically adjusting the mix of data as the model reaches capability milestones.\n\n**Data Engines**\n\nThe infrastructure consists of three specialized, interoperable data engines, each responsible for constructing supervision for a specific capability:\n\n- **Grounding Engine:** Constructs text-image pairs for text-to-image (T2I) generation. This engine uses automated web-scale scraping and filtering, followed by caption generation using large language models (LLMs) fine-tuned for descriptive accuracy. For example, an image of a \"red vintage car on a mountain road\" is paired with a caption generated and verified for relevance and detail.\n\n- **Transformation Engine:** Creates inter-image editing pairs by applying controlled transformations (e.g., object insertion, removal, or style transfer) using automated image editing pipelines. For instance, an original image is edited to add or remove an object, and the corresponding before/after pair is labeled with the specific edit description.\n\n- **Association Engine:** Links images to structured knowledge entities by matching image content to entries in external knowledge bases (e.g., Wikidata). Automated entity recognition models identify objects or scenes in images, which are then mapped to knowledge graph entities, such as linking an image of the Eiffel Tower to its Wikidata entry.\n\n**Caption Experts and Harmonization**\n\nCaption experts are ensembles of LLM-based captioners and entity taggers, further refined by human annotators for high-value or ambiguous cases. These experts harmonize captions and entity tags across tasks, ensuring consistency in descriptions and facilitating cross-task learning. For example, captions generated for T2I data are aligned with those used in editing pairs to maintain semantic coherence. The harmonization module integrates these outputs, standardizing terminology and annotation formats before data is passed to the curriculum scheduler.\n\n**Curriculum Scheduling and Capability Dependencies**\n\nA multi-stage curriculum schedules data exposure based on empirically established capability dependencies. To determine these dependencies, we conduct transfer experiments: small models (n=3 per experiment, each with 500k parameters) are pretrained on one capability (e.g., T2I) and then fine-tuned on another (e.g., editing) using a fixed dataset of 100k samples per capability. Improvements in downstream metrics (e.g., editing precision, entity retrieval accuracy) are measured, and a dependency is established if pretraining yields a statistically significant gain of >5% (p < 0.05, paired t-test with Holm-Bonferroni correction). This threshold balances sensitivity to meaningful transfer while avoiding spurious dependencies; we validated its robustness across multiple tasks and datasets.\n\nCurriculum scheduling proceeds as follows:\n\n- The model is first exposed to data for foundational capabilities.\n- When capability milestones (e.g., FID < 10, CLIP score > 0.28) are reached on held-out validation sets, the scheduler introduces data for more complex capabilities.\n- If evaluation reveals stagnation in a capability (e.g., plateaued FID or CLIP score), the scheduler increases sampling of relevant data.\n- Task composition, visual concept distribution, data quality, and image resolution are progressively adjusted according to predefined thresholds and model performance.\n\n**Data Curation and Quality Assurance**\n\nData curation involves:\n\n- Automated filtering for image quality (BRISQUE < 30, aesthetic scores > 6.0), thresholds chosen based on prior work demonstrating strong correlation with human judgments of image quality (Mittal et al., 2012; Shu et al., 2021).\n- Deduplication (perceptual hashing with Hamming distance < 10) to ensure dataset diversity, following best practices from LAION-5B (Schuhmann et al., 2022).\n- Content safety (NSFW and watermark detectors with > 95% confidence), referencing approaches from OpenAI\u2019s CLIP and LAION datasets.\n- Only images passing these filters are retained.\n\nRecaptioning and entity linking are performed by caption experts, with human review for samples that fail automated confidence thresholds (< 0.85). Strict quality and diversity criteria include minimum caption length (\u2265 8 tokens), vocabulary coverage (at least 90% of a 10,000-word reference lexicon), and entity diversity (minimum 500 unique entities per 100,000 images), ensuring broad representation across concepts.\n\n**Relation to Prior Work**\n\nOur approach builds on and extends prior work in data curation and curriculum learning for generative models. For example, LAION-5B (Schuhmann et al., 2022) and Conceptual Captions (Sharma et al., 2018) focus on large-scale, high-quality text-image pairs, but do not address cross-task harmonization or capability dependencies. Curriculum GANs (Karras et al., 2018) and staged training in DALL-E 2 (Ramesh et al., 2022) introduce curriculum learning for generative models, but use fixed-stage training and do not empirically model dependencies between generative skills. Our key differences include:\n\n- **Explicit modeling of capability dependencies:** Unlike prior pipelines that treat each task in isolation, we empirically establish and leverage dependencies between generative skills through transfer experiments.\n- **Cross-task harmonization:** Our harmonization module ensures consistent annotation across tasks, whereas previous work often uses task-specific annotation schemes.\n- **Dynamic curriculum scheduling:** We adapt data exposure based on real-time model progress, rather than fixed-stage training.\n- **Broader evaluation:** Our infrastructure supports and is evaluated on a wider range of generative tasks (text-to-image, editing, knowledge association) within a unified framework.\n\n**Evaluation**\n\n*Experimental Setup:* We train 6B-parameter models on datasets of 200M images, with 10% reserved for validation. Human evaluation involves 12 raters, each scoring 500 images per model, with inter-rater reliability (Cohen\u2019s \u03ba) of 0.81. All quantitative results are averaged over 3 independent runs; 95% confidence intervals are reported.\n\n*Evaluation Metrics and Reporting:* For text-to-image, we report Fr\u00e9chet Inception Distance (FID; lower is better, measures distributional similarity to real images) and CLIP score (higher is better, cosine similarity between image and prompt embeddings). For editing, we use human-rated editing precision, defined as the average rater score (1\u20135 scale, where 1 = \"no visible edit,\" 5 = \"edit is precise and matches instruction exactly\") for how well the edit matches the instruction. For knowledge association, we report entity retrieval accuracy (percentage of test images for which the correct entity is retrieved from a knowledge base).\n\n*Statistical Analysis:* For each metric, we use paired t-tests to compare our model to each baseline on the same set of prompts or images. To control for multiple comparisons, we apply the Holm-Bonferroni correction. We also monitor for potential confounding factors, such as differences in data preprocessing between models, by standardizing preprocessing pipelines and reporting any deviations.\n\n*CPI-Bench Benchmark and Baselines:* We evaluate on CPI-Bench, a comprehensive benchmark covering text-to-image generation, image editing, and knowledge association tasks. CPI-Bench includes metrics such as FID and CLIP score for text-to-image, editing precision for transformation tasks, and entity retrieval accuracy for knowledge association. Baselines\u2014Stable Diffusion v1.5, Imagen, and DALL-E 2\u2014are chosen for their strong performance and widespread adoption in large-scale image generation, providing a rigorous comparison across all CPI-Bench tasks.\n\n*Quantitative Evaluation:* For text-to-image, our 6B-parameter model achieves an FID of 6.2 \u00b1 0.2 (vs. 8.1 \u00b1 0.3 for Stable Diffusion) and a CLIP score of 0.312 \u00b1 0.004 (vs. 0.295 \u00b1 0.005). For knowledge association, entity retrieval accuracy on CPI-Bench is 78% \u00b1 1"}}